# Bounded, like env.gbnf and for the same reason: an unbounded root is a
# grammar that cannot end, so the sampler never reaches a stop and the model
# keeps writing. Observed live 2026-08-15 -- three attempts, each a complete
# and correct Makefile followed by "# Even more concise version:" and another
# copy, every one dying on n_predict.
#
# The bound has to be tight enough that a COMPLETE file fits inside n_predict,
# not merely finite: at 24 items the same run still rambled to the limit, three
# `clean:` targets deep. Fourteen items is .PHONY plus six targets and the
# blank lines between them -- and a file that needs more than that is a
# scaffolded project, where the targets come from ProjectSpec instead.
root       ::= item{0,14}
item       ::= comment | assignment | rule | "\n"
comment    ::= "#" [^\n]{0,120} "\n"
assignment ::= name " "* ("=" | ":=" | "?=" | "+=") [^\n]{0,200} "\n"
rule       ::= name ":" [^\n]{0,120} "\n" recipe{0,8}
recipe     ::= "\t" [^\n]{0,200} "\n"
name       ::= [A-Za-z_.] [A-Za-z0-9_./-]*
